Sorry for the silence at my end. I've had to move on to other parts of the firmware that I am currently writing.

@avisconti I have switched to using the `zephyr,concat-buf-size` property and it appears to work beautifully. So far I only have one other I2C device and that still works correctly too. Eventually this product will have 5 I2C devices, but I am yet to get around to implementing the others yet. I suspect that worst-case I will have to set `zephyr,concat-buf-size` to be large enough for the largest write used across all I2C devices.

@jfischer-no I also mildly agree that the fix that I am proposing here isn't the best solution to the problem. Now that I know of the existence of the `zephyr,concat-buf-size` property, I can see how that is a better workaround. My main concern is that Zephyr's 'out of the box' experience right now isn't fantastic and that the workaround for this issue is not obvious to the end user. Ideally this could be somehow 'fixed' once and for all (although I suspect that is impossible), but as a minimum I think it would help if the end user could be warned if they have a possibly susceptible situation. However, this PR isn't the right place to discuss how this might be achieved.
 
> > * If other slaves are not affected, why not setting it by default in the yaml binding to some value?
> 
> What value would that be? Should all instances of Nordic TWIM pay the cost of supporting ST sensors that are only rarely present?

@pabigot While the focus of this PR has been ST sensors, surely any I2C device that expects a single write transaction is susceptible? (without extensive research, we don't know if/how many devices that may be). Anywhere where a device driver author has used `i2c_burst_write` on the understanding that it results in a single transaction is at risk. Since the `i2c_burst_write` documentation doesn't explicitly state how it behaves (at least historically, before the warning was added), it is impossible to know what each device driver author's intent was when they wrote the driver.

